    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Robertson (1959a) first addressed the problem of optimal allocation of effort in the half-sib design, the objective being to minimize the sampling variance of the intraclass correlation (defined in Equation 18.21). Assuming that the constraint on the investigator is the total number of individuals that can be measured, $ T = Nn $, the optimal family size is $ \\widehat{n} \\simeq 1/\\tau_{\\mathrm{PHS}} \\simeq 4/h^2 $. Since $ h^2 $ has an upper limit of one, this result clearly indicates that half-sib experiments with family sizes smaller than four should be avoided. Substituting $ 1/\\tau_{\\mathrm{PHS}} $ for $ n $ in Equation 18.21, the expected standard error of the intraclass correlation under an optimal design is",
      "source_page": 578,
      "printed_page": 558,
