    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "As in the case of parent-offspring analysis, the optimal experimental design depends upon the heritability of the trait, but some general recommendations can still be made. Figure 18.2 shows the relationship between SE( $ h^{2} $) and n under two fairly extreme values of $ h^{2} $ and for two values of T = Nn. If $ h^{2} $ is relatively large there is a pronounced increase in SE( $ h^{2} $) as the family size exceeds the optimal value. However, when $ h^{2} $ is small, SE( $ h^{2} $) is very insensitive to the design, provided that n is greater than approximately 20. Thus, with no information on $ h^{2} $ prior to analysis, the most broadly satisfactory recommendation that can be made is to use family sizes on the order of 5 to 20.",
      "source_page": 579,
      "printed_page": 559,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter18:p013:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        66.0,
